          "text": "1) Answer\n\nBased on her official legislative record, votes, and public statements as a Member of Congress since 2019, there is strong evidence that Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez consistently acts on and advocates for human rights:\n\n- She is an original cosponsor of the Dignity for Detained Immigrants Act (118th Congress), which seeks to set humane detention standards, end family detention, increase oversight and accountability, and create a presumption of release or least-restrictive conditions for people in immigration custody. Her cosponsorship is recorded on Congress.gov, and the bill’s companion text describes these human-rights–centric provisions. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/2760/cosponsors))\n\n- She voted Yea on the Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act (House Roll Call 412, Dec. 8, 2021), a measure aimed at blocking goods made with forced labor in Xinjiang from entering U.S. markets—explicitly a human-rights protection against state-sponsored forced labor. The House Clerk’s official roll shows “Ocasio-Cortez — Yea.” ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2021412))\n\n- She cosponsored the Puerto Rico Self-Determination Act (H.R. 2070, 117th Congress), which recognizes Puerto Ricans’ right to determine their political status—an exercise of the human right to self-determination. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/2070?utm_source=openai))\n\n- She has used congressional oversight to highlight treatment of migrants as a human-rights concern, testifying under oath about abusive conditions she observed in U.S. detention facilities (e.g., lack of potable water and degrading treatment), and calling those conditions “manufactured cruelty.” ([cbsnews.com](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/house-oversight-committee-holds-hearing-on-treatment-of-migrants-at-the-border/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- In foreign-policy contexts, she has repeatedly framed protection of civilians and adherence to humanitarian law as paramount. For example, she led and joined letters and statements calling for a Gaza ceasefire to protect children and civilians, urged compliance with U.S. human-rights and humanitarian laws, and called for withholding offensive support when such standards are violated—all positions grounded in human-rights principles. ([ocasio-cortez.house.gov](https://ocasio-cortez.house.gov/media/press-releases/24-members-congress-call-biden-administration-establish-ceasefire-protect?utm_source=openai))\n\n- She has backed domestic civil-rights reforms consistent with human rights, including supporting the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act and cosponsoring it—legislation to curb excessive force, strengthen accountability, and address discriminatory policing. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1280/actions?utm_source=openai))\n\nTaken together—immigration-detention reform, opposition to forced labor, the right of peoples to self-determination, oversight to prevent inhumane treatment, protection of civilians in armed conflict, and civil-rights policing reform—these are concrete, official acts consistent with valuing human rights.\n\n2) Trustworthy sources considered\n\n- Source: Congress.gov (Library of Congress)\n  - Reasoning 1: It is the U.S. Congress’s official legislative database, maintained by the Library of Congress; it provides primary-source records (bill texts, histories, cosponsors). \n  - Reasoning 2: For this prompt, it directly documents Rep. Ocasio-Cortez’s cosponsorships and the human-rights content of the cited bills, such as H.R. 2760 (Dignity for Detained Immigrants Act), H.R. 2070 (Puerto Rico Self-Determination Act), and H.R. 1280 (George Floyd Justice in Policing Act). ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/2760/cosponsors))\n\n- Source: Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives (clerk.house.gov)\n  - Reasoning 1: It is the official keeper of House votes; its roll-call pages are authoritative, primary-source records.\n  - Reasoning 2: It confirms AOC’s individual Yea vote on the Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act (Roll Call 412), which is directly relevant to human-rights policy. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2021412))\n\n- Source: Representative Ocasio-Cortez’s official House website (press releases)\n  - Reasoning 1: This is the official channel for her formal statements as a sitting Member of Congress; it is authoritative for her positions and words, though it is, by nature, from her office.\n  - Reasoning 2: It records her statements and letters emphasizing civilian protection and compliance with humanitarian law in Gaza, showing how she frames issues explicitly in human-rights terms. ([ocasio-cortez.house.gov](https://ocasio-cortez.house.gov/media/press-releases/24-members-congress-call-biden-administration-establish-ceasefire-protect?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: CBS News (reporting on House oversight testimony)\n  - Reasoning 1: Major U.S. news outlet with editorial standards and established fact-checking.\n  - Reasoning 2: Their coverage of her sworn testimony about detention conditions provides third-party documentation of her human-rights–focused oversight and advocacy. ([cbsnews.com](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/house-oversight-committee-holds-hearing-on-treatment-of-migrants-at-the-border/?utm_source=openai))\n\n3) Not-trustworthy sources considered\n\n- None. I relied on primary congressional records and a major-network news report; I did not base conclusions on sources I deemed not trustworthy.",
